---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatJsonCode from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatJsonCode.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="json-to-go">
  <DevFormatJsonCode client:load target="go" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Go-specific decisions the generator makes</h2>
    <h3>Initialisms</h3>
    <p>
      Go&rsquo;s naming convention capitalises initialisms in full, and <code>go vet</code> and the
      linters after golint enforce it. The JSON to Go generator turns <code>user_id</code> into
      <code>UserID</code>, not
      <code>UserId</code>; <code>api_url</code> becomes <code>APIURL</code>; <code>html_body</code>
      becomes <code>HTMLBody</code>. The recognised set covers ID, URL, URI, API, HTTP, HTTPS, JSON,
      XML, HTML, SQL, UUID, IP, TLS, SSH, CPU, RAM, UTC, EOF, DB, OS and TTL.
    </p>
    <h3>Numeric types</h3>
    <p>
      Whole numbers become <code>int64</code>, not <code>int</code>, because <code>int</code> is 32-bit
      on some platforms and JSON integers often exceed that. A millisecond timestamp does. Fractional
      numbers become <code>float64</code>, which is what a JSON number is. Narrow them by hand where you
      know the range, and use <code>string</code> plus a decimal library for money.
    </p>
    <h3>Pointers</h3>
    <p>
      A field that was sometimes null becomes a pointer, so you can tell &ldquo;absent or null&rdquo;
      from the zero value. This matters more in Go than in most languages: without it, <code>0</code>,
      <code>""</code> and <code>false</code> look the same as a missing field. Slices and maps can
      already be nil and are never wrapped.
    </p>
    <h3>Struct tags</h3>
    <p>
      Each field has a <code>json:"…"</code> tag with the original key. Go&rsquo;s case-insensitive
      fallback matching fails when two keys differ only in case, and it hides the wire format in the
      source.
    </p>

    <h2>omitempty, and when to remove it</h2>
    <p>
      Fields missing from some records get <code>,omitempty</code>, so marshalling back to JSON leaves
      them out and does not write a zero value. Decide carefully if you keep it: omitempty also drops
      <code>0</code>, <code>""</code>, <code>false</code> and empty slices, so a field where zero has a
      meaning disappears from your output without a warning. To send an explicit zero and also leave out
      an absent value, use a pointer and remove omitempty.
    </p>

    <h2>Decoding safely</h2>
    <p>
      Two useful habits with the generated structs:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>decoder.DisallowUnknownFields()</code> turns an unexpected key into an error, so you
        learn when the API changes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>decoder.UseNumber()</code> keeps numbers as strings until you convert them. This avoids
        the float64 rounding that damages large integer IDs.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Struct field ordering and memory</h2>
    <p>
      Fields are in the order they appear in your JSON, so the struct is easy to compare with the
      payload. Go pads struct fields for alignment, so on a hot path with millions of instances you can
      reduce memory by putting the largest fields first. Do this only where needed. In almost all cases,
      readability is worth more.
    </p>

    <h2>The output is a package main starter</h2>
    <p>
      The file declares <code>package main</code>, so it compiles as it is for a quick check. Change it
      to your real package name before you add it to a project. Split large outputs into one file per
      logical group, not forty structs in one file.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
